# Line endings, settled once.
#
# Every tracked text file lives in the repository as LF and checks out as LF on
# every platform. Without this file the checkout is at the mercy of each
# machine's core.autocrlf: Windows defaults to true, so LICENSE arrived with
# CRLF while src/lib/capytools/license-text.ts — generated from that file at
# build time and committed — carried the endings of whatever machine last ran
# the generator. tests/license-text.test.ts compares the two byte for byte, so
# a Windows clone failed a test Linux CI passed, and "fixing" it locally by
# re-running the generator flipped the failure onto CI instead.
#
# eol=lf wins over core.autocrlf, so the fix belongs here and not in anyone's
# git config. A checkout that already holds CRLF needs one renormalisation pass
# to pick this up:
#
#   git rm -r --cached . -q && git reset --hard HEAD
#
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts keep CRLF rather than inheriting the rule above: .bat
# and .cmd parsing is the one place it still matters in practice.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf

# Assets are binary — state it, so `text=auto` is never asked to guess.
*.png   binary
*.ico   binary
*.icns  binary
*.webp  binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
